1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:07,730
Yeah. And we're specifically focusing on why government officials, meaning you, the people who actually run these programs, should use a

2
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:12,010
walkthrough instead of just looking at, you know, a simple list of fields.

3
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:18,270
Right. Because just reviewing static fields often results in systems that are, um, fragmented or siloed.

4
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:26,650
Exactly. Or completely duplicated. You end up with data having to be rekeyed, and it leaves your program locked into one supplier

5
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:28,830
and incredibly costly to change.

6
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:30,630
Yeah, nobody wants that. No.

7
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:35,650
But, um, to avoid it, you need to simulate the actual screens, right?

8
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:37,930
Because that provides a perfect shared picture.

9
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:38,650
It does.

10
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:43,710
It saves a lot of time and prevents all those costly changes down the line for your minister.

11
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:50,490
Okay, so wait, why can't officials just, I don't know, review mock-ups in a presentation tool like a standard slide deck?

12
00:00:51,210 --> 00:00:54,830
Well, because a drawing only shows what a designer hoped would happen.

13
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:55,670
Oh, I see.

14
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:56,030
Yeah.

15
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:59,330
And if someone, you know, hand corrects a printed page,

16
00:00:59,970 --> 00:01:02,910
that page no longer shows what the builder will actually build.

17
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:05,250
Right. It's basically a fantasy at that point.

18
00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:06,390
Precisely.

19
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:11,630
So to solve this, your systems require a genuine walkthrough.

20
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:14,390
Okay, and what exactly does that look like?

21
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:20,870
Uh, it consists of simple web pages, literally one for each screen linked in the exact order.

22
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:22,690
And what about errors?

23
00:01:23,510 --> 00:01:28,090
The failures just act as side paths. But the key here is that nobody draws it.

24
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:30,770
Wait, really? Nobody draws the screens?

25
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:40,490
No. A program produces it directly from the written screens, and, uh, every single page must carry a stamp indicating the exact screens, their version and their date.

26
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:46,090
Okay, that makes sense. So to see this in practice, let's look at the example of Progressa.

27
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:52,370
Yes, and just to be clear, Progressa is a completely fictional country, but it perfectly illustrates the method.

28
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:53,690
Right. Fictional country.

29
00:01:54,790 --> 00:01:58,630
So their goal in this example was to apply for a provisional license.

30
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:04,890
Yeah, and that walkthrough involved five pages, plus side pages for failures and, um, two endings.

31
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:07,410
And the stamp at the top.

32
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:12,330
The stamp read: Version one point two, fourteen November 2026.

33
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:19,830
So the registrar of PHEQA, the builder, and the head of the registration desk all sat and clicked through it together.

34
00:02:20,790 --> 00:02:22,430
I do have to push back a little here, though.

35
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:27,730
Did clicking through it like that actually reveal anything a basic list of fields wouldn't have caught?

36
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:29,030
Oh, it absolutely did.

37
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:30,030
Really? Like what?

38
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:35,650
Well, on page two, the head of the desk realized the fee wasn't actually shown until page four.

39
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:36,730
Oh, wow.

40
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:38,970
Yeah, you'd never see that on a spreadsheet.

41
00:02:39,170 --> 00:02:39,710
Exactly.

42
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:45,390
And then on a side page, an open policy question emerged about canceled institution names.

43
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:48,570
Like whether they're still considered held or not.

44
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:49,210
Right.

45
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:55,810
And then, uh, by page five, the builder learned that the application number is not the same as the register number.

46
00:02:56,610 --> 00:02:59,710
Okay, so doing it this way creates a shared language.

47
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:06,710
The policy side recognizes its counter, the technical side sees exactly what to build, and they agree on the exact same version.

48
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:10,390
Yes, because they are literally experiencing it together.

49
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:14,690
So how is this clicking process officially executed in a program?

50
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:18,190
The walkthrough recording will show seven specific steps.

51
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:19,550
Okay. Step one:

52
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:25,850
open the screen record of the goal, apply for a provisional license at the version to be accepted.

53
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,890
Step two: open page one of the walkthrough in a browser.

54
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:30,410
Got it.

55
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:34,530
Step three: click the button to apply for a provisional license.

56
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:35,790
Step four:

57
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:39,110
choose a kind of institution and go on. Right.

58
00:03:39,890 --> 00:03:41,650
Step five: go on to confirm.

59
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:45,030
Step six: confirm and pay. Simple enough.

60
00:03:45,410 --> 00:03:48,950
And step seven: go on to the end of the path which is not yet recorded.

61
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:52,690
So the core takeaway for anyone running these systems is pretty clear.

62
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:55,170
Ask for the walkthrough, not a drawing.

63
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:56,450
Never a drawing.

64
00:03:56,590 --> 00:04:03,090
Right. Check the version stamp on every single page, and most importantly, let the official, not the builder, hold the mouse.

65
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:10,590
Consider how simply shifting the physical control of the mouse from the builder to the official completely shifts the balance of power,

66
00:04:11,230 --> 00:04:15,730
ensuring the policy side truly owns the final service before anything is actually built.

